<section class="band band--rule" aria-labelledby="sticker">
	<p class="kicker">06 — the die-cut vocabulary</p>
	<h2 id="sticker">Printed, cut, and sitting above the page</h2>
	<p class="lede">A sticker is a printed thing, cut out of a band of paper and laid on a dark textured bed. Four things make it read as an object, and dropping any one of them stops it being a sticker: the band, the keyline, the tilt, and the shadow.</p>

	<div class="scroll">
	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Value</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>--sticker-band-ratio</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--sticker-band-ratio`,
				stickerProp('--sticker-band-ratio'),
			)}</td><td>The band as a proportion of the object's <em>width</em>: ${esc(
				(Number(stickerProp('--sticker-band-ratio')) * 100).toFixed(1),
			)}%, the midpoint of the 6–8% measured on real die-cut stickers. Kept as a raw ratio so a canvas or SVG renderer can apply the same rule outside CSS.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>--sticker-band</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--sticker-band`,
				stickerProp('--sticker-band'),
			)}</td><td>The same rule in CSS. <code>cqw</code> <em>is</em> "a percent of the container's width", so this expresses the measurement rather than approximating it. Needs <code>container-type: inline-size</code> on an ancestor — an element cannot query itself.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>--sticker-band-fixed</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--sticker-band-fixed`,
				stickerProp('--sticker-band-fixed'),
			)}</td><td>For anywhere container units are unavailable. Not a lesser option — it is the right answer on a button.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>--sticker-keyline</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--sticker-keyline`,
				stickerProp('--sticker-keyline'),
			)}</td><td>The default die-cut stroke. It equals <code>--focus-width</code>, which is what makes a focused die-cut component shift by zero pixels. It is also the boundary that survives forced-colours mode, where the shadow is dropped by the OS.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>--sticker-keyline-bold</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--sticker-keyline-bold`,
				stickerProp('--sticker-keyline-bold'),
			)}</td><td>Large display die-cuts only. On a button it reads as a mistake.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>--sticker-keyline-heavy</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--sticker-keyline-heavy`,
				stickerProp('--sticker-keyline-heavy'),
			)}</td><td>The selected carousel sticker at roughly 2×, and nothing smaller.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>--sticker-keyline-hair</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--sticker-keyline-hair`,
				stickerProp('--sticker-keyline-hair'),
			)}</td><td>Dividers and inner rules. Never a die-cut boundary — that is the whole distinction.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>--sticker-radius</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--sticker-radius`,
				stickerProp('--sticker-radius'),
			)}</td><td>A die-cut never has a sharp corner: the blade has a radius and the paper tears if it does not.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>--tilt-rest</code></td><td class="num">${V(
				`css:${P.sticker}#--tilt-rest`,
				stickerProp('--tilt-rest'),
			)}</td><td>${esc(motion.transforms.rest.notes)}</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>

	<p class="notes">The wordmark applies the same rules with the <em>letterform</em> as the die-cut object, so the basis is its ink height: ${V(
		`${P.metrics}#/at100px/inkHeight`,
		metrics.at100px.inkHeight,
	)} at 100px, measured off the shipped WOFF2 by <code>build-wordmark.mjs</code> rather than assumed. Band ${V(
		`${P.metrics}#/dieCutRatios/band`,
		metrics.dieCutRatios.band,
	)}, keyline ${V(`${P.metrics}#/dieCutRatios/keyline`, metrics.dieCutRatios.keyline)}, shadow offset ${V(
		`${P.metrics}#/dieCutRatios/shadowDx`,
		metrics.dieCutRatios.shadowDx,
	)} / ${V(`${P.metrics}#/dieCutRatios/shadowDy`, metrics.dieCutRatios.shadowDy)} of that height. ${V(
		`${P.metrics}#/dieCutRatios/note`,
		metrics.dieCutRatios.note,
	)}</p>
</section>

